 This install applies to "Rescue on Fractalus!", an Amiga port of the 1985
 Atari 8-bit game by the Lucasfilm Games Division.

 It is an unofficial, non-commercial fan project, not affiliated with or
 endorsed by Lucasfilm. The port is a faithful 1:1 reimplementation, rebuilt
 from the original game rather than emulated.

 Nothing has to be copied from an original disk: the whole game is one
 self-contained executable, and it ships inside this archive.


 Requirements:
 -------------
 This install requires WHDLoad version 17.0 or better to be in the path. It
 can be found in the WHDLoad package (http://www.whdload.de/).

 The game needs about 1.2 MB of memory for itself and for the emulated
 operating system, of which 384 KB must be chip RAM. In practice that means a
 2 MB machine. PAL only.

 The game requires an installed A500 Kickstart 1.3 image.
 The kickstart image must be located in the directory "Devs:Kickstarts" and
 must be named "kick34005.A500". The image can be extracted from an A500 with
 tools like 'GrabKick' or similar which can be found on aminet.
 If the image is of wrong version or altered in any way WHDLoad will quit with
 an appropriate message.
 Additionally the file "kick34005.A500.RTB" must be installed in the same
 directory as the kickstart image. It can be found in the archive
 "util/boot/skick346.lha" on aminet.


 Why a Kickstart image is needed:
 --------------------------------
 Most WHDLoad installs patch a game that killed the operating system. This one
 is the other way round. The port is an ordinary AmigaDOS program that uses the
 OS to take the display over -- it opens graphics.library, allocates its chip
 memory with AllocMem, saves and restores the view with LoadView, and takes the
 vertical-blank interrupt vector and the CIA-A keyboard vector for the duration.
 WHDLoad leaves no operating system at all, so the Slave boots a real Kickstart
 into WHDLoad's memory and runs the game inside it, unmodified. Kickstart 1.3
 rather than 3.1 because the game is 1.3-clean and the 1.3 image is half the
 size.


 Quitting:
 ---------
 Press the left mouse button. The game shuts down, restores the machine and
 returns you to Workbench.

 *** Careful: it quits IMMEDIATELY, with no confirmation, at any point in the
 game -- including mid-mission. A stray click ends the run and the score with
 it. Keep the mouse somewhere you will not knock it. ***

 The WHDLoad QuitKey (F10, changeable with the Tooltype "QuitKey=") also works,
 but only on a 68010 or better -- WHDLoad reads it through the relocated vector
 base register, which a plain 68000 does not have. On a stock A500 the left
 mouse button is the only way out.


 Controls:
 ---------
	Arrow keys		Steer (pitch and roll)
	Left Shift		Fire torpedo
	- / =			Thrust down / up
	L			Land
	B			Boosters (return to the mother ship)
	A			Air lock -- open it when a pilot knocks
	S			Systems -- engines and shields on/off
	Esc			Pause / unpause
	F1			Start
	F2			Level select
	F3			Demo mode
	Help			Abort mission and restart

 Thrust is on the two keys just right of 0, because those are the physical
 positions of the Atari 800's "< -" and "> =" thrust keys.

 A joystick in port 1 works too: directions steer, button 1 fires, button 2
 lands and launches.

 At the Standby screen press F1 to launch, or F2 (or arrow up) for the level
 selector, where arrow up/down chooses a starting level.

 Once you have landed, press S to shut the engines and shields down. A downed
 pilot will not leave cover while they are on -- and the energy field will kill
 anything that comes close.

 And check who is knocking before you open the air lock.


 The manual:
 -----------
 The file "Manual" installed alongside the game is the original Lucasfilm
 manual, transcribed in full: the story, the mission briefing, what every
 cockpit instrument does, and the pilot rankings. Read it if you want to
 actually understand the dashboard.


